== Reinforcement Learning for Visual Sequential Decision-Making

The idea of treating visual perception as a sequential decision process has deep roots in the reinforcement learning literature. Mnih et al. @mnih2014ram introduced the Recurrent Attention Model, which learns a policy for sequentially attending to informative image regions via REINFORCE. By casting _where to look_ as a series of discrete spatial decisions, this work established the foundational principle that perception need not be a single feed-forward pass but can instead unfold as a goal-directed sequence of actions. Caicedo and Lazebnik @caicedo2015active extended this principle to object localization, training a DQN agent that iteratively transforms a bounding box through discrete spatial actions (translate, scale, aspect-ratio change) until it converges on a tight detection. Their formulation demonstrated that _spatial refinement_ is naturally expressible as a Markov decision process over image observations. Mathe et al. @mathe2016rl further developed RL-based detection by learning both a search policy and a _stopping condition_, explicitly addressing the exploration-exploitation tradeoff inherent in deciding when an object has been sufficiently localized.

Subsequent work broadened the scope of RL-driven visual reasoning. Bellver et al. @bellver2016hierarchical proposed a hierarchical detection framework in which a DQN agent performs _coarse-to-fine_ zoom-in actions, recursively subdividing an image to focus computational effort on promising regions. This multi-scale decomposition strategy parallels the sub-mask decomposition that our V2 system learns. Zhu et al. @zhu2017target introduced target-driven visual navigation, conditioning an RL policy on both the current visual observation and a goal specification, a paradigm that anticipates our goal-conditioned prompting where the agent must reconcile the current segmentation state with the desired mask. Park et al. @park2018distort framed image enhancement as an MDP with interpretable discrete actions (adjust contrast, shift color, crop), showing that complex visual transformations decompose into learnable sequential steps.

Collectively, these works establish the principles that our SAM prompt optimization inherits. Sequential spatial actions over image observations @mnih2014ram @caicedo2015active, learned stopping criteria @mathe2016rl, coarse-to-fine hierarchical refinement @bellver2016hierarchical, goal-conditioned visual policies @zhu2017target, and discrete interpretable action spaces @park2018distort all find direct analogs in our formulation of iterative SAM prompting as an MDP.
